.UE


.PP
.SS
1. Simple migration:

.PP

.EX
rdbm <args>  migrate
.EE
.P
.B
OR
.P
.EX
rdbm -c config.cfg migrate
.EE
.PP
Where <args> OR config.cfg:
.TS
tab(@), left, box;
c | c
rB | l.
Field@Value
_
db_host@IP address or host name
db_name@database name
db_user@database user
db_password@database user password
.TE
.RE

.TP
This will perform simple migration of all files found in current directory by the execution order
.B
.TP
  (P)->(S)->(V)->(R)->(after)
.P
Separately on every found version.
.P

.TS
tab(@), left, box;
c | c | c | c | c
rB | rB | l| l| l.
#@Code@Script@Type@Value
_
1@P@PreDeploy@SQL@immutable
2@S@Schema@YAML@mutable, (D)ata will be deployed there
3@V@Version@SQL@immutable
4@R@Re-runable@SQL@mutable
_
5@A@after@SQL@optional, see '--after', '--after_each'
.TE
.RE

.B
immutable:
- will executed only once on target database, see '--checksum'
.P
.B
mutable:
- will be execute once change detected

.PP
See:
.EX
rdbm help migrate
.EE
- For more information

.P
.SS
2. Migration with ETL:
.P
Assume we have a YAML file with two tables: table_a and table_b with 'table.etl.file' fields set to CSV file:
.P
.EX
rdbm \fB\-\-etl=skip \fB\-\-snapshot_data=table_a \fB\-\-source_dir=./sql migrate
.EE
.P
Do not use CSV for table_a. Apply table_b.etl.file(etc) from ./sql/ directory.

.P
.EX
rdbm \fB\-\-etl=clean \fB\-\-snapshot_data=table_a \fB\-\-source_dir=./sql \fB\-\-snapshot_to=./data  migrate
.EE
.P
 Use 'clean' ETL rule for table_a (delete extras), and 'apply' for table_b. Use ./data/ directory to locate CSV files.

.P
.EX
rdbm \fB\-\-etl=force \fB\-\-snapshot_data=table_a  migrate
.EE
.P
 Use 'force' ETL rule for table_a (update if exist), and 'apply' for table_b. Use ./ directory to locate YAML and CSV files.

.P
.EX
rdbm \fB\-\-etl=ignore migrate
.EE
.P
 Do not fail, if required file is absent, otherwise use 'apply' rule.

.P
.EX
rdbm \fB\-\-etl=skip migrate
.EE
.P
 Do not perform any ETL task for all tables

.P
.EX
 rdbm migrate
.EE
.P
 Default ETL rule 'apply' will use for table_a and table_b.
 Records from CSV files fromn table.etl.file and relative to current directory.
 The CSV file required to have a header.

  Use '\fB\-\-dry_run' to avoid DB change.

  See more options in 'rdbm help' command.

.P
.SS
3. Snapshot with ETL:
.P

rdbm \fB\-\-snapshot_data=table snapshot

Perform a DB schema dump into a '\fB\-\-snapshot_to' file (or dir) in yaml or json format.

Use '\fB\-\-snapshot_version' to define a D<number>__db_name.yaml.

See '\fB\-\-snapshot_data' to define list of tables to dump for ETL(extract) with YAML schema.

The '\fB\-\-etl' along with '\fB\-\-snapshot_data' can use in 'rdbm migrate' to perform ETL(load) into target database.

Notice: There is a limited amount of column information that will be converted from pg_catalog and information_schema
to fully match an input format of the manual yaml schema created.

Therefore, the dump schema snapshot and yaml input file may differ in some cases.

Some types such as arrays are not support for a snapshot.

